Description: fftools/ffmpeg_filter: continue the output of agapfill when a filter graph is rebuilt
 ffmpeg builds a new filter graph when the parameters of an input change
 mid-stream, for example when an AC-3 stream changes from 5.1 to stereo.
 The agapfill filter of the new graph started from nothing. With its
 start option it wrote the leading silence a second time: a source of
 10.05 s that begins at 2 s came out as 19.07 s. A gap at the change
 stayed a hole, because only the old filter knew where the audio had
 stopped, and an overlap at the change made the output step backward.
 .
 The filter gains two commands. position reports where its output
 stands, and resume makes a filter continue from such a report. Before
 send_frame() replaces a graph, it notes the position of every agapfill
 filter. configure_filtergraph() makes the filter of the same name in
 the new graph resume from it. A filter that took no frame reports
 nothing, so its successor starts afresh and still writes the leading
 silence. That is the case after a seek whose discarded frames hold the
 change.
 .
 The filter writes a gap one frame of silence for each frame its output
 asks for, and holds the frame after the gap, and the frames behind it,
 until then. ffmpeg freed a graph with those frames in it when another
 input of the same graph changed, for example the frame size of a video.
 A gap of 30 s under way at such a change lost 4,096 to 16,384 samples
 and the rest of the silence. The pending command reports the frames a
 filter has not output yet, and send_frame() now asks the outputs behind
 each agapfill filter for frames until none is pending. It does not ask an
 output whose consumer has finished, for example after -frames, which
 would only discard the rest of the gap.
